# Does the residue direction 5 KEEPS grow? — measured 2026-09-08
#
# forge/syncer/tests/push_chain.rs::measure_whether_the_residue_direction_5_keeps_grows
#
# Eight IDENTICAL rounds. Each round: one accepted push (so live content
# grows too — the denominator has to be honest), one MIXED push (one ref
# accepted, one refused, ONE pack), one wholly-refused push.
#
# Direction 5 does not touch the pinning rule. It changes which packs
# step 5 NAMES; once named, strict object coverage governs and a base
# rebuild's --all output never covers the dead objects, so the input
# stays named. The question is whether what it keeps is bounded.

## rep 1
round  packs    named B  redundant   d5 drops   d5 KEEPS  keeps %
    0      2      19628       9767          0       9767     100%
    1      4      39285      29038       9502      19536      67%
    2      6      58824      48191      19003      29188      60%
    3      8      78365      67345      28506      38839      57%
    4     10      97905      86500      38009      48491      56%
    5     12     117446     105655      47513      58142      55%
    6     14     136989     124811      57018      67793      54%
    7     16     156533     143967      66522      77445      53%

## rep 2 / rep 3 (final row)
    7     16     156403     143840      66519      77321      53%
    7     16     156426     143849      66518      77331      53%

## The slope, rep 1

round 1: +9769 B   round 2: +9652   round 3: +9651   round 4: +9652
round 5: +9651 B   round 6: +9651   round 7: +9652

CONSTANT. +9,651 B per mixed push, forever, in all three reps.
+2 packs per round, both pinned: one from the mixed push (direction 5
keeps it) and one from the wholly-refused push (direction 5 drops it).

## The answer

IT GROWS — linearly, without bound, one pack per mixed push. Nothing
collects it: direction 5 changes the naming, not the pinning, and the
only collector is a base rebuild's --all, whose output cannot cover what
it dropped. Direction 5 lowers the SLOPE; it does not make it zero.

The PERCENTAGE converges instead of growing, because live content grows
too — here to ~53% and still falling toward 9651/(9651+9502) = 50.4%,
which is just this rig's 1:1 mix of mixed to wholly-refused pushes. That
ratio is a property of the WORKLOAD, not of direction 5: the 78-81%
figure in residue-direction5-20260908.log came from a 1:3 mix. Neither
number is a prediction for a real repository, and the rate of mixed
pushes on one has not been measured.

Unattributed residue at the end: 0 B in every rep.
